      "name": "Knowledge",
      "values": [
        {
          "id": "none",
          "level": 0,
          "label": "no knowledge of graph parameters",
          "short": "no knowledge",
          "definition": "Agents know no graph parameter ($n$, $m$, $\\Delta$, $D$)."
        },
        {
          "id": "n-delta",
          "level": 1,
          "label": "$n$ and $\\Delta$ are known",
          "short": "knows $n$, $\\Delta$",
          "definition": "Every agent knows $n$ and $\\Delta$ (upper bounds suffice where stated)."
        }
      ],
      "origin": "Whether agents know global parameters is inherited from message passing, where nodes typically know $n$ or an upper bound on it, and possibly $\\Delta$ and $D$; Kshemkalyani, Kumar, Molla and Sharma set the agent-based model against this and asked for algorithms that rely on no such knowledge, \"neither exact nor an upper bound\" [Kshemkalyani2024AgentLE]. Knowing $n$ and $\\Delta$ tells an agent how long to wait: in the arbitrary-start minimal dominating set algorithm of Chand, Molla and Sivasubramaniam every robot waits $\\kappa n\\Delta$ rounds before leader election begins [Chand2023Cover], and the introductory maximal independent set algorithm assumes both $n$ and $\\Delta$ [Pattanayak2024MIS] (as reported by [Kaur2026MIS] and [Kshemkalyani2024AgentLE]). Kshemkalyani et al. state the trade-off: solutions without knowledge \"are more resilient to network changes and device faults\", but \"algorithm design becomes challenging since devices may not know how long to run a procedure to guarantee a solution\" [Kshemkalyani2024AgentLE]. Later papers remove the assumption by first dispersing with termination detection and electing a leader, after which a spanning tree lets the agents compute $\\Delta$ and the largest identifier themselves [Chand2026Triangle]; knowing only that largest identifier $L$ is a weaker assumption, recorded on results rather than as a level of this axis [Chand2025Trees]. For leader election both levels are catalogued: without knowledge [Kshemkalyani2024AgentLE] [Chand2026MDS], and faster with $n$ and $\\Delta$ known [Kshemkalyani2026Leader]."
    }
  ],
